#---
# purpose: "the Keyword module over a list of {atom, value} pairs, with duplicates behaving as Elixir's do"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# # keyword_surface
#
# A keyword list is an ORDERED list of 2-tuples with atom keys, and duplicates
# are allowed. That is the whole difference from Map, and it decides every
# function's behaviour -- which is why the fixture uses a list that HAS a
# duplicate (`:a` appears twice) rather than a tidy one:
#
#   * `get/2` and `fetch/2` answer with the FIRST match, so the later `:a` is
#     shadowed, not merged;
#   * `get_values/2` returns both;
#   * `delete/2` removes ALL entries for a key, `delete_first/2` removes one;
#   * `put/3` deletes every entry for the key and prepends, so it both makes
#     the key unique AND moves it to the front;
#   * `keys/1` and `values/1` keep order and keep duplicates.
#
# In Elixir this module is `:lists.keyfind` and friends. fz has no such BIFs, so
# every function is a plain recursion over the list -- which is why this module
# needs no allocating primitives at all.
#
# RENDERING is part of the parity: Elixir inspects a proper list whose every
# element is a two-tuple with an atom key as `[a: 1]`, not `[{:a, 1}]`, and only
# then. The decision is made from the VALUES, so `[{:a, 1}, {2, 3}]` still
# prints as tuples.
def main() do
  kw = [{:a, 1}, {:b, 2}, {:a, 3}]
  dbg(Keyword.get(kw, :a))
  dbg(Keyword.get(kw, :z, :none))
  dbg(Keyword.fetch(kw, :b))
  dbg(Keyword.fetch(kw, :z))
  dbg(Keyword.get_values(kw, :a))
  dbg(Keyword.keys(kw))
  dbg(Keyword.values(kw))
  dbg(Keyword.has_key?(kw, :b))
  dbg(Keyword.delete(kw, :a))
  dbg(Keyword.delete_first(kw, :a))
  dbg(Keyword.put(kw, :a, 9))
  dbg(Keyword.put_new(kw, :c, 4))
  dbg(Keyword.take(kw, [:a]))
  dbg(Keyword.drop(kw, [:a]))
  dbg(Keyword.pop(kw, :a))
  dbg(Keyword.keyword?(kw))
  dbg(Keyword.keyword?([1, 2]))
end
